Struct-ctor argument corrupts next slice argument (TRISC codegen)
===================================================================

Pinned 2026-04-29. The TRISC `evalAndPushArg` struct/enum branch was
forgetting to pop any "extra" stack used during `genExpr(arg)` before
allocating its own copy slot. When a struct **constructor expression**
appeared as a non-final positional argument, the constructor's
materialisation temporary shifted every subsequent argument's stack
offset, and the callee read garbage where it expected its later args
(slice `len`, `int`, etc.).

Real-world bite: in `std/regex/vm.lsysl`, `exec()` calls
`vm.add_thread(current, Thread(0, init_saves), visited, sp, input_len)`.
The `Thread(...)` constructor was the second arg; `visited` was the
third. Inside `add_thread` the `len(visited)` field read as 0, so
`visited[t.pc]` immediately bounds-trapped — that was the entire 96-OOB
`std/regex/vm` cluster on the `--backend trisc` test runner.

Fix in `SyslTriscCodegen.scala` mirrors the slice/string/scalar branches
of the same function: pop the genExpr "extra" before allocating the
struct's copy slot. r1 still points to the source bytes — they live in
the just-popped stack region until our copy reads them, and the only
thing emitted between the pop and the read is register arithmetic.

    module sysl.tests.struct_ctor_arg

    struct ST
        a: int
        b: int

    callee(_pad: []int, t: ST, vis: []bool) -> int
        return len(vis)

    #test
    test_ctor_arg_before_slice() -> unit
        val _l = new [1]int
        val _v = new [3]bool
        val r = callee(_l[:1], ST(0, 0), _v[:3])
        assert(r == 3, "len lost across struct-ctor arg")
